# Patterns for check-anonymity.sh -- one extended regex per line.
#
# COPY THIS TO .anon-patterns AND FILL IT IN. That file is gitignored, because a
# tracked file listing the strings that identify you is itself the leak.
#
# Case-insensitive. Include every form a string can take: the surname alone, the
# handle, the handle with digits, the email local part, the ORCID digits with and
# without hyphens, and the institution in both languages.
#
# A line reading exactly
#
#     [review]
#
# splits the file in two. Patterns ABOVE it are hard failures: a hit is a leak
# and the script exits non-zero. Patterns BELOW it are reported but do not fail,
# for strings that are usually innocent in prose -- the word "Zenodo" in a
# sentence about where to deposit is not a leak, but you still want to see every
# place it occurs before you mint a DOI under your own name.
#
# Put anything that names a person, an account or an institution above the
# marker. Put archive and platform names below it. A checker that always fails
# gets ignored, and a checker that is ignored is worse than none.

# --- people -------------------------------------------------------------
# surname
# firstname
# surname-of-each-coauthor

# --- accounts and hosts -------------------------------------------------
# your-github-handle
# your-github-handle[0-9]+
# the public specification repository name

# --- identifiers --------------------------------------------------------
# [0-9]{4}-[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{4}   # any ORCID at all
# your-student-or-staff-number
# your-email-local-part

# --- institutions -------------------------------------------------------
# university name
# university name in its other language
# campus city, if it narrows to one group

# --- things that leak the artifact's provenance -------------------------
# a personal domain used in a figure footer
# a lab or project name that appears in only one group's work

# ========================================================================
[review]
# Below here: reported, but does not fail the check.
# ========================================================================

# --- archives that would carry your name if you deposit under it --------
zenodo
figshare
osf\.io
orcid

# Local filesystem paths. A tracked file containing /home/<username>/ leaks a
# username as surely as a surname does, and it is easy to paste in by accident
# when documenting a working copy. Add your own login name here.
# your-login-name
/home/[a-z0-9_-]+/
